{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 10, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "*highlighted section part 1*  \nRaymond III was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. He was born in 1140 and was the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His early life was marked by political instability in the Crusader states. He inherited the county at the age of twelve after his father was assassinated. Due to his youth a regency was established led by his mother and later by his uncle Baldwin III of Jerusalem. During this time Tripoli was a key Christian stronghold on the Levantine coast and often faced threats from Muslim powers especially under the leadership of Nur ad-Din and later Saladin.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*  \nRaymond III became an influential figure in the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He was married to Eschiva of Bures and later to Stephanie of Milly. His marriage alliances strengthened his position among the nobility. He served as regent of Jerusalem twice. The first time was during the minority of Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy. Raymond was appointed regent in 1174 and held the position until 1177. His second regency began in 1183 after Baldwin IV became too ill to rule. Raymond was a cautious and pragmatic leader who often clashed with the more aggressive military orders like the Knights Templar. He favored diplomacy over open warfare and sought to avoid unnecessary conflict with Muslim neighbors.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*  \nRaymond III played a central role in the events leading to the Battle of Hattin in 1187. He was accused by some contemporaries of being too accommodating to Saladin and even of treason. These accusations stemmed from his secret negotiations with the Muslim leader. Raymond was present at the battle but did not command the Christian army directly. After the catastrophic defeat he fled to Tripoli and spent his final years trying to rebuild the county. He died in 1187 shortly after the fall of Jerusalem. His death marked the end of a long and complex reign defined by political maneuvering and survival in a hostile environment. Raymond III remains a controversial figure whose legacy is debated by historians. Some view him as a realist who preserved Christian holdings through diplomacy while others see him as indecisive and overly cautious. His rule reflected the fragile nature of the Crusader states during their final decades.", "logprobs": [{"token": "*", "logprob": -5.1973900554003194e-05}, {"token": "highlight", "logprob": -0.0013335392577573657}, {"token": "ed", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " section", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " part", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " ", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "*", "logprob": -3.2305197237292305e-05}, {"token": "  \n", "logprob": -0.018150663003325462}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -1.2755313036905136e-05}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.006557493936270475}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.006636602338403463}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.06345247477293015}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -1.0013530300057027e-05}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0009490515221841633}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.00014304091746453196}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -4.768370445162873e-07}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -3.325883881188929e-05}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -1.311301275563892e-06}, {"token": " until", "logprob": -0.313278466463089}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -0.0023292573168873787}, {"token": " death", "logprob": -3.099436753473128e-06}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -2.0503786799963564e-05}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -3.3378546504536644e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "8", "logprob": -3.0994367534731